// **A misspelled constraint is a syntax error, not a rule that silently does not run.** The parser
// reads field attributes against one table (`FIELD_ATTRS`) and refuses anything not in it, so this
// names every attribute a field takes -- which is also how a reader finds the one they meant.
//
// `#[deprecated]`'s years of parsing-and-doing-nothing is the argument for paying this cost: an
// attribute the compiler accepts and no generator reads is indistinguishable from one that works.
struct E {
    #[not_blak]
    name: string
}

fn main() {
    print(1)
}
